<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shimabara Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1637">1637</a>–<a href="/years/1638">1638</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shimabara Rebellion was an uprising of peasants, many of them Christians, and masterless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> on the Shimabara Peninsula and the Amakusa islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> in 1637–1638, provoked by heavy taxation, famine and persecution. Led nominally by the youth Amakusa Shirō, the rebels held Hara Castle until it was stormed by a shogunal army of over 100,000 men. Its suppression was followed by the expulsion of the Portuguese and a stricter ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion.jpg" alt="Shimabara Rebellion" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shimabara-Battle-Map-c17th-century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Shimabara had been the domain of the Christian Arima clan, which was moved out in 1614 and replaced by the anti-Christian Matsukura. The new lord, Matsukura Shigemasa, hoped to rise in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> by sponsoring costly projects, among them work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a> and a planned invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, and built a new castle at Shimabara. He placed a heavily disproportionate tax burden on his people and persecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> strictly, policies his heir Matsukura Katsuie continued. The Amakusa Islands suffered similar treatment under the Terasawa family, and the region held many unemployed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, former retainers of the Arima, Konishi and other houses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Discontented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> and impoverished peasants plotted in secret, and the uprising broke out on 17 December 1637 with the assassination of the magistrate Hayashi Hyōzaemon. A charismatic 16-year-old, Amakusa Shirō, emerged as leader. The rebels besieged Tomioka and Hondo castles and Shimabara Castle but were forced back by armies from neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> domains. They gathered at the ruins of Hara Castle, former seat of the Arima, and used weapons and provisions plundered from the Matsukura storehouses.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s allied army, under Itakura Shigemasa, besieged Hara Castle. The swordsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyamoto-musashi">Miyamoto Musashi</a> was present as an adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadatoshi">Hosokawa Tadatoshi</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, and the ship de Ryp bombarded the castle before withdrawing. Itakura was killed in a failed advance and replaced by Matsudaira Nobutsuna. On 3 February 1638 a surprise attack killed 2,000 warriors of the Hizen Domain, but the castle's food and ammunition then ran out. Troops of the Kuroda clan stormed the outer defences on 12 April 1638, and the rebels were routed on 15 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> executed an estimated 37,000 rebels and sympathizers. Amakusa Shirō's head was displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, and Hara Castle was burned and buried with the dead. Katsuie was beheaded in Edo, the only daimyō decapitated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, and Terasawa Katataka's lands were confiscated. Portuguese traders, suspected of helping the rebels, were expelled, and national seclusion was stricter by 1639. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> survived only underground.</p><p>Migrants from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> resettled the peninsula, whose towns had lost most of their men, and every inhabitant was registered with a local temple. The rebellion is often portrayed as Christian, but the academic consensus is that it was driven chiefly by peasant anger at misgovernment, with Christians joining later. Apart from local peasant risings, it was the last large-scale armed clash in Japan until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shimabara Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Shimabara Rebellion</b> of 1637-1638, Japan under the Tokugawa saw no major warfare for more than two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The shogunate made the Buddhist temples an arm of the state: after the <b>Shimabara Rebellion</b> of 1637–1638, every household had to belong to a temple, which certified each year that its members were not Christians, and the registers built on these certificates became, in effect, a register of…</blockquote><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Social Hierarchy of Tokugawa Japan</a></li></ul></section>
